   CITATIONS, verified against the primary sources (2026-08-13):
   - White et al., 2019, Sci Rep 9:7730. The 120-minutes-in-nature dose.
   - Garrett et al., 2023, Sci Rep 13:2209, 14,998 visits across 18 countries.
     "Only coastal sites were associated with significantly higher levels of visit
     happiness." This finding was previously mis-attributed here to White et al.
     2021 and over-scoped as the biggest boost of ANY natural environment. Both
     were wrong: the first author is Garrett (White is second of fifteen), and the
     comparison is WITHIN water settings, against the grand mean of blue-space
     types. Green space is not the comparator, so the copy now says "among water
     settings". White et al., 2021, Sci Rep 11:8903 finds green, inland-blue and
     coastal-blue visits ALL positively associated with wellbeing; it carries no
     coastal-outperforms finding and is no longer cited here.
   - Cooley et al., 2020, Clin Psychol Rev 77:101841. Meta-synthesis, 38 studies,
     322 practitioners and 163 clients. Qualitative: how outdoor sessions are
     experienced, not efficacy.
   - Thompson Coon et al., 2011, Environ Sci Technol 45(5):1761. Systematic review,
     exercise in nature vs indoors.
   NOT PUBLISHED: any "outdoor therapy is at least as effective as indoor" claim.
   The only head-to-head trial found (Dickmeyer et al., 2025) is a 37-person pilot
   its own authors call preliminary. See CONTENT_EVIDENCE.md line 37.

