{% call s.prose({ width: "narrow" }) %}
  <p>Is there anything to this beyond a nice walk? Actually, yes. In <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-019-44097-3">a study of nearly 20,000 people</a>, spending at least two hours a week in natural settings was associated with noticeably better health and well-being (White et al., 2019, <em>Scientific Reports</em>). And among water settings, the coast is the one that stands out: in <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-28544-w">a later study of about 15,000 visits across 18 countries</a>, the coast was the only kind of water setting where people reported significantly higher happiness during the visit (Garrett et al., 2023, <em>Scientific Reports</em>).</p>
  <p>What about the walking part itself? That research is younger and mostly qualitative, so we hold it loosely. A review of 38 studies found practitioners and clients describe outdoor sessions as more mutual and less pressured than a room (Cooley et al., 2020), and exercising in nature has been linked to more revitalization and less tension, anger, and low mood than the same exercise indoors (Thompson Coon et al., 2011). Promising, not settled, and we would rather say so.</p>
  <p>None of that is a promise about your particular Tuesday. It just means the shoreline is a well-worn place to do this kind of work, and the map has been walked before.</p>
